# Binned colour gradients: scale_steps

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_colour_steps()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_steps.html), which covers
`scale_colour_steps2()`, `scale_colour_stepsn()`, `scale_fill_steps()`, `scale_fill_steps2()`
and `scale_fill_stepsn()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code
first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what
it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

The steps scales are the binned counterparts of the gradient scales: they cut a continuous column
into bins and give each bin one colour from a gradient. `scale_colour_steps()` runs from a low
colour to a high one, `scale_colour_steps2()` diverges from a low colour through a middle one to a
high one, around a midpoint, and `scale_colour_stepsn()` takes its colours from as many as it is
given; the `fill` versions do the same for fill. Giggly has all six,
`Giggly.Plot.scale_color_steps/2`, `Giggly.Plot.scale_color_steps2/2`,
`Giggly.Plot.scale_color_stepsn/2`, `Giggly.Plot.scale_fill_steps/2`,
`Giggly.Plot.scale_fill_steps2/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_fill_stepsn/2`, with ggplot2's options,
and reads them back with colour steps, as `guide_coloursteps()` draws at its defaults. A plot is
described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The
setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws
a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`.

## A binned gradient

The examples draw from a table of random numbers R makes after `set.seed(1)`.

```r
set.seed(1)
df <- data.frame(
  x = runif(100),
  y = runif(100),
  z1 = rnorm(100)
)

# Use scale_colour_steps for a standard binned gradient
ggplot(df, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = z1)) +
  scale_colour_steps()
```

The cell writes out `df`'s `x`, `y` and `z1` as R made them, each number to the last digit R
holds. Each point takes the colour of its bin, from `#132B43` at the lowest to `#56B1F7` at the
highest, each bin's colour the gradient's at the bin's middle.

